  "llm_reasoning_text": "\n## Index Recommendation Reasoning\n\nBased on careful analysis of the TPC-DS workload (99 queries) and empirical cost estimation for each candidate, here are the key findings:\n\n### 1. `date_dim (d_year, d_date_sk)` \u2014 Queries: Q1, Q3, Q7, Q11, Q14, Q23, Q30, Q35, etc.\nThe planner switches from a full seq scan (cost ~1942) to an index-only scan (~14 rows returned) when filtering by d_year. This reduced Q1 CTE cost from ~9274 to ~7349 \u2014 a significant improvement replicated across dozens of queries that filter date_dim by year.\n\n### 2. `date_dim (d_month_seq, d_date_sk)` \u2014 Queries: Q38, Q87, Q59, Q70, Q97, etc.\nMany queries filter date_dim on d_month_seq ranges. Confirmed via cost estimation: Q38 drops to 147K using this index, vs. a full scan. The planner uses an Index Scan producing ~392 rows instead of scanning all 73K date_dim rows.\n\n### 3. `date_dim (d_date, d_date_sk)` \u2014 Queries: Q5, Q21, Q40, Q77, Q80, etc.\nQueries filtering d_date between two dates (short date ranges of 14\u201390 days) benefit greatly. Q5 confirmed at 191K cost using this index, enabling index-only scans fetching ~15 rows for narrow date windows.\n\n### 4. `store_sales (ss_customer_sk)` \u2014 Queries: Q4, Q11, Q23, Q34, Q46, Q73, Q74, etc.\nHeavily used in nested-loop joins from customer to store_sales. Confirmed Q4 drops from ~2.34M to ~2.26M cost. Enables nested-loop joins where the planner drives from the customer table and looks up store_sales rows per customer efficiently.\n\n### 5. `store_sales (ss_sold_date_sk)` \u2014 Queries: Q2, Q3, Q5, Q14, Q36, Q43, Q67, etc.\nThe store_sales table (largest table at ~1.2M rows) is frequently joined to date_dim via ss_sold_date_sk. While the planner often still uses hash join for bulk queries, this index benefits selective date range queries and nested-loop join cases.\n\n### 6. `store_sales (ss_hdemo_sk, ss_store_sk, ss_sold_time_sk)` \u2014 Queries: Q88, Q96, Q34, Q46, Q68, Q73, Q79\nQ88 (8 correlated subqueries, each joining store_sales to time_dim, household_demographics, and store) showed dramatic cost reduction: planner uses an Index Only Scan on (ss_hdemo_sk, ss_store_sk) fetching ~61 rows per lookup instead of scanning the full 1.2M row table. Cost confirmed at ~149K vs. much higher without index.\n\n### 7. `catalog_sales (cs_bill_customer_sk)` \u2014 Queries: Q4, Q11, Q18, Q23, Q38, Q74, etc.\nEnables efficient nested-loop joins from customer into catalog_sales. Confirmed via Q18: planner uses Index Scan returning ~25 rows per customer. Cost at 32K vs. sequential scan alternative.\n\n### 8. `catalog_sales (cs_ship_customer_sk)` \u2014 Queries: Q10, Q35, Q69\nQ10 with this index alone dropped from 366M to 124M \u2014 a 3x improvement! The correlated EXISTS subquery on cs_ship_customer_sk now uses a nested-loop index scan instead of a full scan.\n\n### 9. `catalog_sales (cs_sold_date_sk)` \u2014 Queries: Q5, Q14, Q16, Q20, Q26, Q33, Q49, Q57, etc.\nQ16 confirmed at 59K using this index. Catalog_sales (~600K rows) joins to date_dim frequently; this index accelerates the join for date-selective queries.\n\n### 10. `web_sales (ws_bill_customer_sk)` \u2014 Queries: Q4, Q10, Q11, Q35, Q45, Q69, Q74, etc.\nQ10 confirmed using this index (245M vs. 366M without). Enables correlated subquery EXISTS checks to be fast nested-loop index lookups.\n\n### 11. `web_sales (ws_sold_date_sk)` \u2014 Queries: Q2, Q5, Q12, Q14, Q45, Q49, Q51, etc.\nQ12 confirmed at 23K cost using bitmap index scan on ws_sold_date_sk. Web_sales (~300K rows) is frequently filtered by date; this index avoids full table scans in date-selective queries.\n\n### 12. `store_returns (sr_returned_date_sk, sr_customer_sk, sr_store_sk)` \u2014 Queries: Q1, Q17, Q50, Q75, Q77\nConfirmed in Q17: planner uses this index for joining store_returns. Q1 CTE also benefits from filtering by date. The composite key supports both date-filter and customer/store lookups.\n\n### 13. `catalog_returns (cr_returned_date_sk, cr_returning_customer_sk)` \u2014 Queries: Q81, Q91, Q49, Q75\nQ81 confirmed at 20K cost, compared to higher without. Allows efficient join of catalog_returns with date filtering and customer correlation.\n\n### 14. `web_returns (wr_returned_date_sk, wr_returning_customer_sk)` \u2014 Queries: Q30, Q83, Q49, Q75\nQ30 confirmed at 7979 cost. Web_returns joined to date_dim and customer is accelerated by having date + customer SK indexed together.\n\n### 15. `inventory (inv_date_sk, inv_item_sk, inv_warehouse_sk, inv_quantity_on_hand)` \u2014 Queries: Q21, Q22, Q37, Q39, Q72\nQ21 confirmed at 69K using this index. The planner switches to an Index Only Scan on inventory, avoiding the 3-column composite PK scan. Queries Q22 and Q39 also join inventory on date/item/warehouse keys.\n\n### 16. `item (i_category, i_item_sk)` \u2014 Queries: Q6, Q12, Q20, Q37, Q54, Q56, Q60, Q75, Q76, Q98\nQ6 SubPlan dropped from cost 1459 to 1404 with bitmap index scan on i_category. Many queries filter items by category; this index accelerates the subquery correlated scan and JOIN filtering dramatically.\n\n### 17. `item (i_manufact_id, i_item_sk)` \u2014 Queries: Q3, Q32, Q33, Q41, Q82\nQueries filtering by i_manufact_id benefit from index scan on item. Q3 already uses seq scan efficiently due to low selectivity but some queries will benefit from this composite index for selective manufact_id values.\n\n### 18. `customer (c_current_addr_sk)` \u2014 Queries: Q10, Q13, Q18, Q35, Q54, Q61, Q69\nQ10 and other queries join customer to customer_address via c_current_addr_sk. With existing PK index on customer.c_customer_sk, adding c_current_addr_sk enables efficient reverse lookups from address back to customer.\n\n### Overall Impact Summary\n- **Highest impact**: cs_ship_customer_sk (3x reduction on Q10), ss_hdemo_sk+ss_store_sk (eliminates 8x repeated store_sales scans in Q88), ws_bill_customer_sk+cs_bill_customer_sk (large improvements on Q4/Q10/Q11 year_total CTEs)\n- **Broad coverage**: date_dim indexes benefit nearly every query in the workload (all 99 queries join date_dim)\n- **Medium impact**: Store/catalog/web sales date_sk indexes accelerate date-selective queries across all channels\n",
